# Neo Space parameters (docs/specs/neo-space.md). The order changes only with a version (machines.md §3.2): songs and presets
# store values by position. Regenerate: python3 tools/gen-machine-params.py Machines/NeoSpace
namespace NeoBuzz.Space
class NeoSpaceMachine
summary Convolution reverb with zero latency: nine rooms of our own (small room, ambience, drum room, chamber, plate, hall, large hall, cathedral, tunnel: image-source early reflections, then a filtered-noise tail with its own decay in three bands) or any wave of the song as the room (mono or stereo, up to 10 s), with pre-delay, length (a trim with a fade), low and high cut, width, reverse, Mix, Amount (0 to 200 %) and Output. A new room is prepared in the background and crossfaded in over 50 ms. Use it for real-sounding space, or to put a sound into any recorded or made space; best on a send at 100% wet.

group Room
Room       "Room"          0 9 5      enum Small_room Ambience Drum_room Chamber Plate Hall Large_hall Cathedral Tunnel Wave | The impulse response: one of nine rooms made by our own code, or Wave (the song's wave in the Wave slot)
    how Chooses the room: nine built-in rooms from a small room to a cathedral and a tunnel, or Wave, which uses a wave of the song as the room.
    sound Small rooms add closeness, a chamber or a hall add depth, a plate is bright and dense, a cathedral and a tunnel ring for seconds, and a wave puts the sound inside that wave.
Wave       "Wave"          0 199 0    int 1 wave                       | The wave slot (1 to 200) used when Room is Wave; the song saves the wave, this only names its slot
    how Picks the slot of the song's wavetable that is the room when Room is Wave, from 1 to 200.
    sound Any recording works as a room: a clap or a burst of noise gives a natural space, a drone or a voice gives a strange, coloured wash. An empty slot falls back to the hall.
PreDelay   "Pre-delay"     0 1000 40  lin 0 250 ms 1                  | Silence before the reverb starts (the response's first reflection arrives this late)
    how Sets the gap before the reverb starts, from 0 to 250 ms.
    sound A longer gap keeps the dry sound clear in front of the reverb and adds a sense of distance.
Length     "Length"        0 1000 1000 lin 5 100 % 0                  | How much of the response is used: the rest is cut with a fade
    how Sets how much of the room's response is kept, from 5 to 100 %; the end is faded out so the cut cannot click.
    sound Shorter values cut the tail and make the room smaller and tighter, 100 % keeps the whole natural decay.
group Tone
LowCut     "Low cut"       0 1000 0   freq 20 1000                     | High-pass on the response (12 dB/oct, twice); at its lowest it is off
    how Removes low frequencies from the reverb, from 20 Hz to 1 kHz; the lowest setting is off.
    sound Raising it keeps the reverb from muddying the bass and thins it out.
HighCut    "High cut"      0 1000 1000 freq 1000 20000                | Low-pass on the response (12 dB/oct, twice); at its highest it is off
    how Rolls off high frequencies of the reverb, from 1 to 20 kHz; the highest setting is off.
    sound Lowering it darkens the reverb so it sits further behind the dry sound.
Width      "Width"         0 2000 1000 lin 0 200 % 0                  | Stereo width of the response: 0 is mono, 100 % as it is, 200 % twice the side
    how Sets the stereo width of the reverb, from 0 % (mono) to 200 %.
    sound Low widths focus the reverb in the middle, high widths spread it far past the speakers.
Reverse    "Reverse"       0 1    0   switch                          | Plays the response backwards: the tail swells up into the sound
    how Plays the room's response backwards.
    sound The reverb builds up from nothing and stops at its loudest point, like a swell that pulls back into the note.
group Output
Mix        "Mix"           0 1000 300 pct                             | Dry/wet (100 % for a send); the dry sound is never delayed
    how Blends the dry sound with the reverb from 0 to 100 %.
    sound Use a low value as an insert, or 100 % when the reverb sits on a send.
Amount     "Amount"        0 2000 1000 lin 0 200 % 0                  | Effect strength: 100 % = the settings as shown, 0 % = bypass (bit for bit the input), 200 % = twice the wet level
    how Scales how much reverb there is: the wet level goes with Amount (and the dry level makes room for it up to 100 %). 0 % is bypass, 100 % your settings, 200 % doubles the wet level (6 dB more).
    sound Turn it down to hear the room fade away, up to push the reverb further forward.
Output     "Output"        0 1000 500 db -24 24                       | Level of the reverb (the wet signal)
    how Sets the level of the reverb in dB, from -24 to +24.
    sound It makes the reverb louder or quieter without changing the room.
